WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.874
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:08.595
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:15.692
Unit two: Argumentation & Debate. Today you will add force and focus to your arguments with emphatic structures.

4
00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:18.494
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:22.794
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.430
Rebuttal.

7
00:00:25.417 --> 00:00:29.355
Rebuttal means a response that attempts to disprove an opposing claim.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.250
Her rebuttal dismantled the opposition's central statistic.

9
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.653
Your turn. Say: Rebuttal.

10
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.397
Rebuttal.

11
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.279
Counterargument.

12
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:46.525
Counterargument means a reason put forward against your position.

13
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:50.219
Anticipating the counterargument strengthens your own case.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.376
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

15
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.525
Counterargument.

16
00:00:58.283 --> 00:00:59.656
Logical fallacy.

17
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.064
Logical fallacy means a flaw in reasoning that makes an argument invalid.

18
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.356
Attacking a speaker's character is a classic logical fallacy.

19
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.125
Your turn. Say: Logical fallacy.

20
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.653
Logical fallacy.

21
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:19.371
Persuade.

22
00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:23.962
Persuade means to cause someone to accept a belief through reasoning.

23
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.548
The data persuaded even the most sceptical judges.

24
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.081
Your turn. Say: Persuade.

25
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:34.760
Persuade.

26
00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:36.402
Concede.

27
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:41.100
Concede means to admit, often reluctantly, that a point is valid.

28
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:45.041
I concede that the policy is expensive, but it is essential.

29
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.488
Your turn. Say: Concede.

30
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:52.311
Concede.

31
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.074
Evidence.

32
00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:59.184
Evidence means facts or information indicating whether a claim is true.

33
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:02.491
Anecdotes are not a substitute for evidence.

34
00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:05.794
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

35
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:09.774
Evidence.

36
00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:11.564
Moderator.

37
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.699
Moderator means a neutral person who manages a debate.

38
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:19.792
The moderator intervened when the speakers interrupted each other.

39
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:22.687
Your turn. Say: Moderator.

40
00:02:25.533 --> 00:02:26.465
Moderator.

41
00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:28.009
Verdict.

42
00:02:29.017 --> 00:02:31.678
Verdict means a formal judgement or decision.

43
00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:34.387
The panel reached a unanimous verdict.

44
00:02:34.983 --> 00:02:37.311
Your turn. Say: Verdict.

45
00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:40.959
Verdict.

46
00:02:41.717 --> 00:02:42.555
Premise.

47
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.676
Premise means a proposition on which an argument is based.

48
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:50.618
If the premise is false, the conclusion collapses.

49
00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:53.683
Your turn. Say: Premise.

50
00:02:56.533 --> 00:02:57.407
Premise.

51
00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.163
Fallacious.

52
00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.485
Fallacious means based on mistaken or misleading reasoning.

53
00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:06.617
The claim that everyone agrees is fallacious.

54
00:03:07.217 --> 00:03:09.950
Your turn. Say: Fallacious.

55
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.813
Fallacious.

56
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.204
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:24.315
Visal, the judges said your rebuttal was the strongest of the tournament. What was your approach?

58
00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:32.783
I avoided emotional appeals. It is evidence that truly persuades an audience, so I built every point on data.

59
00:03:33.883 --> 00:03:36.254
But you also conceded a point, didn't you?

60
00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:45.262
Yes, deliberately. What conceding a minor point does is make you appear fair, which makes your main argument more credible.

61
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:50.602
And how did you deal with their claim that 'everyone knows' uniforms improve discipline?

62
00:03:51.717 --> 00:04:00.685
I pointed out that it was an appeal to popularity, a logical fallacy. It wasn't their conclusion I attacked, but their premise.

63
00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:03.427
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:04.283 --> 00:04:06.747
Grammar focus: Emphatic structures.

65
00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:08.223
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.682
It is evidence that truly persuades an audience.

67
00:04:12.583 --> 00:04:15.349
What conceding a point does is make you appear fair.

68
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.341
It wasn't their conclusion that I attacked, but their premise.

69
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:28.618
Emphatic structures move the key information into focus. 'It is X that …' highlights one element; 'What … is …' delays the key idea for dramatic effect.

70
00:04:29.383 --> 00:04:33.290
Be careful! Don't say: It is evidence what persuades people.

71
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:37.918
Say: It is evidence that persuades people.

72
00:04:38.218 --> 00:04:39.962
Use 'It is … that', not 'what'.

73
00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:43.628
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:45.968
The Anatomy of a Debate.

75
00:04:46.268 --> 00:04:50.590
Skilled debaters know that volume and confidence rarely win an argument on their own.

76
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.895
It is evidence that truly persuades an audience, particularly when it is drawn from credible, independent sources.

77
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:04.417
An effective rebuttal targets the premise of the opposing case rather than merely contradicting its conclusion.

78
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:11.496
Paradoxically, conceding a minor point can increase a speaker's credibility, since it signals fairness.

79
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:18.377
What judges ultimately reward is not rhetorical flair, but reasoning free from logical fallacies.

80
00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:23.933
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:24.783 --> 00:05:27.758
It is evidence blank truly persuades an audience.

82
00:05:28.058 --> 00:05:30.899
A, what. B, that. C, which it.

83
00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:35.945
The answer is B: that.

84
00:05:37.083 --> 00:05:41.675
Transform: 'Their premise was weak.' → What blank was their premise.

85
00:05:41.975 --> 00:05:45.454
A, was weak. B, weak was. C, it was weak.

86
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.081
The answer is A: was weak.

87
00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:56.550
'Everyone believes it, so it must be true' is an example of a blank.

88
00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:00.886
A, rebuttal. B, concession. C, logical fallacy.

89
00:06:04.433 --> 00:06:07.640
The answer is C: logical fallacy.

90
00:06:08.783 --> 00:06:12.199
Which verb means 'admit a point is valid, often reluctantly'?

91
00:06:12.499 --> 00:06:16.025
A, concede. B, persuade. C, moderate.

92
00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:21.531
The answer is A: concede.

93
00:06:22.683 --> 00:06:26.227
According to the reading, why can conceding a point help a speaker?

94
00:06:26.527 --> 00:06:32.909
A, It signals fairness.. B, It ends the debate faster.. C, It confuses the judges..

95
00:06:36.467 --> 00:06:38.989
The answer is A: It signals fairness..

96
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.502
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:46.872
What makes an argument convincing in a debate?

98
00:07:03.683 --> 00:07:05.184
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:05.484 --> 00:07:34.115
I would argue that it is the quality of the evidence that makes an argument convincing. Reliable data and expert sources carry far more weight than emotional appeals. For example, a single well-chosen statistic can undermine an opponent's entire case. Of course, delivery matters too, and a nervous speaker may struggle to persuade. Nevertheless, what judges ultimately remember is whether the reasoning held up.

100
00:07:34.415 --> 00:07:36.160
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.490
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and emphatic structures.

102
00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:46.474
Today's value is respectful disagreement. Disagree politely and listen to others.

103
00:07:46.774 --> 00:07:49.415
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

104
00:07:49.715 --> 00:07:52.878
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

